       🔴 THE PREDICTION THIS STAGE WAS GIVEN WAS REFUTED, AND THE REFUTATION IS THE FINDING. The brief
       expected the 23 CS1734 to be signatures that had DRIFTED away from their own documentation — a
       renamed or removed parameter. Measured: ZERO of 23. Every one of the 23 names a parameter that
       exists, spelled correctly, on a constructor or method of the very same type; all 23 sit in a
       TYPE-level `///` block, where C# has no parameter scope and `<paramref>` therefore cannot resolve
       no matter what it names. It is a SCOPE defect, uniformly, and not a drift defect. No signature in
       this project has drifted from its own doc comment.

       🔴 AND 38 OF THE 75 CS1574 HAD ONE CAUSE, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE COUNT. `cref="Models.X"` is
       correct inside St4i.Connector.Abstractions (where `Models` binds to that assembly's own child
       namespace) and was copied verbatim into five files under St4i.EdgeCore.Drivers, where `Models`
       binds to St4i.EdgeCore.Models instead — a namespace that exists and contains none of those types.
       The spelling resolved on one side of a project boundary and silently failed on the other. That is
       exactly the class of rot §8.1 records as invisible to every text-measuring instrument, and it is
       what the switch was turned on to make visible.

